From c5a43764a60be32acc508913b1758d2491538169 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tomtomnet <tompere8000@proton.me>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 19:00:43 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 19/24] physmem: align memfd-backed RAM so that shmem gives it
 huge pages

shmem (memfd, tmpfs) maps transparent huge pages only where the virtual address and the file
offset agree modulo the huge page size: the mapping is aligned to it. A guest's first
application start after boot went from 1.38 to 0.79 s with shmem huge pages (2026-10-06).

Split out of the 2026-10-12 snapshot of the fork's uncommitted work.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 system/physmem.c | 11 +++++++++++
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)

diff --git a/system/physmem.c b/system/physmem.c
index bd5f41cb02..5295d672cd 100644
--- a/system/physmem.c
+++ b/system/physmem.c
@@ -1734,6 +1734,17 @@ static void *file_ram_alloc(RAMBlock *block,
     if (kvm_enabled()) {
         block->mr->align = MAX(block->mr->align, QEMU_VMALLOC_ALIGN);
     }
+#elif defined(__linux__)
+    /*
+     * shmem (memfd, tmpfs) maps transparent huge pages only where the virtual
+     * address and the file offset agree modulo the huge page size: align the
+     * mapping as anonymous RAM is, so that guest RAM in a memfd can use them
+     * (/sys/kernel/mm/transparent_hugepage/shmem_enabled; QEMU already
+     * madvises guest RAM MADV_HUGEPAGE). With 4 KiB pages every first touch
+     * of guest RAM is a host fault: ~270k of them for a guest's first
+     * Firefox start after boot.
+     */
+    block->mr->align = MAX(block->mr->align, QEMU_VMALLOC_ALIGN);
 #endif
 
     if (memory < block->page_size) {
